        q: "You are leading a payments product at a fintech and your engineering team tells you the PCI DSS compliance module will delay the launch by six weeks. Your CEO wants to ship in two weeks for a key partnership deadline. Walk me through exactly how you handle this.",
        a: `The first thing I do is resist the instinct to immediately escalate or push back on engineering. Six weeks versus two weeks is a significant gap and before I walk into any conversation with the CEO I need to understand what is actually driving that number.\n\nSo I sit down with the engineering lead and I ask three specific questions. First, is the six week estimate for full PCI DSS compliance or for the minimum viable compliance scope that covers our launch use case? Second, are there any components of the compliance work that can run in parallel with other launch prep rather than sequentially? Third, what is the actual risk profile if we launch with a phased compliance approach where certain lower risk flows go live first?\n\nIn my experience in payments, PCI DSS estimates often include buffer because compliance work is genuinely unpredictable. But there is almost always a conversation to be had about scope.\n\nOnce I have that clarity I go to the CEO with three options, not a problem. Option one is we hold the launch date and I negotiate a modified partnership agreement that shifts the deadline by four weeks, framing it as us protecting the partner from compliance exposure as much as protecting ourselves. Option two is we launch a limited version of the product that covers the partnership use case within existing compliance boundaries and treat full launch as a phase two four weeks later. Option three is we bring in a specialized PCI compliance contractor to run parallel workstreams and compress the timeline, and I need budget approval to do that.\n\nWhat I never do is walk into that CEO conversation with just the problem. In fintech, regulatory timelines are a fact of life and leaders expect PMs to show up with structured options, not escalations.\n\nThe partnership deadline matters but shipping a payments product with incomplete PCI DSS compliance is not a calculated risk. It is an existential one. My job is to find the path that protects both.`,
