        q: "You are managing a core banking system migration for a mid size fintech. Three weeks before go live your QA team flags that the data migration scripts are producing reconciliation errors for approximately 2% of customer accounts. Leadership wants to proceed on schedule. How do you respond?",
        a: `I do not proceed on schedule and I say that directly to leadership with the specific reason.\n\nA 2% reconciliation error rate in a core banking migration sounds small until you translate it into accounts. If this fintech has 500,000 customers, 2% is 10,000 accounts with incorrect data going live. In a regulated financial environment, that is not a rounding error. That is a potential RBI reportable incident and a customer trust problem that takes months to recover from.\n\nBefore I walk into that leadership conversation I do two things. First, I ask QA to characterize the errors. Are these 2% of accounts experiencing the same type of error or multiple different error types? Are they concentrated in a specific product type, account vintage, or transaction history pattern? The answer tells me whether this is a single fixable bug or a systemic problem in the migration logic. Second, I get an honest estimate from the data engineering team on how long a fix and re run would take.\n\nIf the errors are concentrated and fixable in five to seven days, my recommendation to leadership is a two week delay with a hard commitment on the revised date. I frame this as protecting the company from regulatory exposure and customer compensation costs that would far exceed the cost of a two week delay.\n\nIf the errors are diverse and the fix timeline is uncertain, I recommend a phased go live where we migrate accounts that reconcile cleanly and hold back the error cohort for a subsequent migration wave. This keeps the project moving while giving the team time to address the complex cases.\n\nWhat I do not do is go live with known data errors and plan to fix them post migration. In core banking, bad data in production compounds. Transactions post against incorrect balances, interest accrues on wrong principals, and the reconciliation effort six months later is an order of magnitude more expensive than the delay today.\n\nLeadership pressure to ship on schedule is real and I take it seriously. But my job is to give them the accurate picture of what shipping with known errors actually costs, not to absorb that pressure and say yes.`,
