        q: "You are a PM at a large public sector bank building a mobile banking app for customers in rural India. Your UX research shows that 60% of your target users cannot read Hindi or English fluently. Your engineering team says adding vernacular language support will push the launch by three months. How do you resolve this?",
        a: `I do not frame this as a launch delay problem. I frame it as a product viability problem.\n\nIf 60% of my target users cannot read the primary language of the app fluently, I do not have a product for them. I have a product for the 40%. Launching on schedule for the wrong audience is not a win, it is a three month head start on building something that will not achieve its adoption targets.\n\nBut before I accept the three month estimate I ask engineering a more specific question. Which specific vernacular languages do we need to support to cover the majority of the 60%? India has enormous linguistic diversity and the answer to that question probably narrows the scope significantly. If 80% of our rural user base in the target geography speaks one of three languages, we can design a phase one that supports those three and plan phases two and three for the remaining languages.\n\nI also ask whether the three month estimate includes building a full translation and localization infrastructure or just adding language support for the highest priority languages. These are very different engineering efforts and conflating them inflates the timeline.\n\nIf after scoping the estimate remains two to three months for minimum viable vernacular support, my recommendation is to delay. I present this to leadership with the adoption projection for a launch that excludes 60% of the target audience versus a launch that includes them. In rural banking, where financial inclusion metrics are often tied to regulatory commitments and government partnership agreements, launching a product that functionally excludes the majority of the target segment creates problems that are harder to solve than a timeline delay.\n\nI would also explore whether we can do a limited pilot launch in a single geography with the vernacular support we can build in six weeks, gather real user data, and use that to pressure test the broader rollout plan. A pilot launch is not the same as delaying. It is learning before scaling.`,
